From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] the launch wake lock outlives a launch on this CPU

While an activity is being started, the activity manager holds a wake lock so
that the device stays awake until the new activity is there (ActivityStack
acquires it whenever it pauses something and is not going to sleep). AOSP gives
up that wake lock after 10 s, a deadline sized for phone hardware, and patch
0012 already multiplies the other deadlines of this kind by
ro.hw_timeout_multiplier.

This one was missed: a cold start of WeRead takes about 17 s on this 996 MHz
single core, so every cold start passed the deadline and logged "Launch timeout
has expired, giving up wake lock!" (twice in two starts, Test V51). With the
screen on, the display's own wake lock keeps the device awake and nothing is
lost; a launch with the screen off - from an alarm or a notification - can
suspend in the middle and finish only at the next wake.

IDLE_TIMEOUT and SLEEP_TIMEOUT in the same file are left as they are. They do
not hold a wake lock: they decide when the system stops and destroys activities
it has not heard from, and on 512 MB waiting four times as long to release them
is a change worth measuring before it is made.

--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/am/ActivityStackSupervisor.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/am/ActivityStackSupervisor.java
@@ -203,7 +203,15 @@
     static final int SLEEP_TIMEOUT = 5 * 1000;

     // How long we can hold the launch wake lock before giving up.
-    static final int LAUNCH_TIMEOUT = 10 * 1000;
+    // PW3: with the factor of ro.hw_timeout_multiplier (ActivityManagerService,
+    // patch 0012). A cold start of a large application takes about 17 s on this
+    // 996 MHz single core (Test V51), so the wake lock meant to keep the device
+    // awake until the new activity starts was given up in the middle of every
+    // one of them. IDLE_TIMEOUT and SLEEP_TIMEOUT above are not changed: they
+    // hold no wake lock, they decide when activities the system has not heard
+    // from are stopped and destroyed, which is worth measuring first.
+    static final int LAUNCH_TIMEOUT =
+            10 * 1000 * ActivityManagerService.HW_TIMEOUT_MULTIPLIER;

     static final int IDLE_TIMEOUT_MSG = FIRST_SUPERVISOR_STACK_MSG;
     static final int IDLE_NOW_MSG = FIRST_SUPERVISOR_STACK_MSG + 1;
